rather than a native HTTP/browser run. No live workroom source, refs, keys, resident or checkout was modified by these probes.\n\nArchitecture: layer 1 gains a bounded lineage reader; layer 6's existing projection fields are read without a schema/profile or projected-byte change; layer 7 gains association and cleanup-report fields. Reusing the kernel-independent Git scan and exporting the unchanged OwnedEdge/AdoptedDecision rules preserves the relevant review binding decisions. The same-head architecture and wire documentation exist, but their complete/unknown/cache contract is not met by the cases above, so approval is withheld. The implementation introduces no gs worktree command, private checkout record, lane-ref writer or durable branch field, and this child cannot close or narrow parent #22168. S2 and S3 remain distinct undelivered outcomes. The note's branch-schema and operational-rollback explanations preserve historical bytes and distinguish source revert from undoing emitted events; no new S2 selector refusal is approved by this review. Preserve the exact stage requests when refining their wording.\n\nSecurity: an unsigned source trailer remains a claim; I examined exact-commit corroboration via effective unretired artifacts and the shared owned edge, foreign/unresolved handling, copied-ident controls, object re-verification and the lack of association-driven admission. The verified source hash seals commit bytes, not the truth of its trailer. Final classifications still protect pending decisions, unknown lifecycle states, refless and unsafe-path checkouts, and the code performs no deletion. The cache/incomplete findings concern truthful bounded reporting; I have not demonstrated unauthorized deletion or a signature bypass. Repair them without turning a missing answer into negative evidence or weakening the existing independent cleanup protections.\n\nSimplification: keep one shared resolution/corroboration rule. Make cache inputs and completion propagation